isCodimAtLeast -- returns true if we can quickly see whether the codim is at least a given number
*************************************************************************************************

  * Usage: 
        isCodimAtLeast(n, I)
  * Inputs:
      * n, an *note integer: (Macaulay2Doc)ZZ,, an integer
      * I, an *note ideal: (Macaulay2Doc)Ideal,, an ideal in a polynomial ring
        over a field, or a quotient ring
  * *note Optional inputs: (Macaulay2Doc)using functions with optional inputs,:
      * SPairsFunction => a *note function: (Macaulay2Doc)Function,, default
        value FunctionClosure[../../../../../Macaulay2/packages/FastMinors.m2:
        217:23-217:42], a function to control how when the codimension of
        minors is computed, default is i->ceiling(1.5^i)
      * PairLimit => a *note number: (Macaulay2Doc)Number,, default value 100,
        the max value to be plugged into SPairsFunction
      * Verbose => ..., default value false
  * Outputs:
      * true if the codimension of I is at least n or null if the function
        cannot tell whether the codimension is at least n

Description
===========

This computes a partial Groebner basis, takes the initial terms, and checks
whether that (partial) initial ideal has codimension at least n. Consider the
following example.  We create an ideal of 15 minors of the matrix myDiff (a
matrix constructed in a way typical of applications).  We would like to verify
that the codimension of this ideal is at least 3.  The built-in codim function
typically does not terminate. However, isCodimAtLeast is normally very fast.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i1 : R = ZZ/127[x_1 .. x_(12)];                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i2 : P = minors(3,genericMatrix(R,x_1,3,4));                                 |
|                                                                             |
|o2 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i3 : C = res (R^1/(P^3));                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i4 : myDiff = C.dd_3;                                                        |
|                                                                             |
|             30      12                                                      |
|o4 : Matrix R   <-- R                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i5 : r = rank myDiff;                                                        |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i6 : J = chooseGoodMinors(15, r, myDiff, Strategy=>StrategyDefaultNonRandom);|
|                                                                             |
|o6 : Ideal of R                                                              |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i7 : time isCodimAtLeast(3, J)                                               |
| -- used 0.00299992s (cpu); 0.00321781s (thread); 0s (gc)                    |
|                                                                             |
|o7 = true                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

The function works by computing gb(I, PairLimit=>f(i)) for successive values of
i.  Here f(i) is a function that takes t, some approximation of the base degree
value of the polynomial ring (for example, in a standard graded polynomial
ring, this is probably expected to be \{1\}).  And i is a counting variable.
You can provide your own function by calling isCodimAtLeast(n, I,
SPairsFunction=>( (i) -> f(i) ), the default function is
SPairsFunction=>i->ceiling(1.5^i)   Perhaps more commonly however, the user may
want to instead tell the function to compute for larger values of i.  This is
done via the option PairLimit.  This is the max value of i to be plugged into
SPairsFunction before the function gives up.  In other words, PairLimit=>5 will
tell the function to check codimension 5 times.

+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i8 : I = ideal(x_2^8*x_10^3-3*x_1*x_2^7*x_10^2*x_11+3*x_1^2*x_2^6*x_10*x_11^2|
|                                                                             |
|               ZZ                                                            |
|o8 : Ideal of ---[x  , x , x , x , x  , x , x , x  , x , x , x , x ]         |
|              127  11   8   1   9   12   6   5   10   2   4   3   7          |
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|-x_1^3*x_2^5*x_11^3,x_5^5*x_6^3*x_11^3-3*x_5^6*x_6^2*x_11^2*x_12+3*x_5^7*x_6*|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|x_11*x_12^2-x_5^8*x_12^3,x_1^5*x_2^3*x_4^3-3*x_1^6*x_2^2*x_4^2*x_5+3*x_1^7*x_|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|2*x_4*x_5^2-x_1^8*x_5^3,x_6^8*x_11^3-3*x_5*x_6^7*x_11^2*x_12+3*x_5^2*x_6^6*x_|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|11*x_12^2-x_5^3*x_6^5*x_12^3,x_8^3*x_10^8-3*x_7*x_8^2*x_10^7*x_11+3*x_7^2*x_8|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|*x_10^6*x_11^2-x_7^3*x_10^5*x_11^3,x_2^8*x_4^3-3*x_1*x_2^7*x_4^2*x_5+3*x_1^2*|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|x_2^6*x_4*x_5^2-x_1^3*x_2^5*x_5^3,-x_6^3*x_11^8+3*x_5*x_6^2*x_11^7*x_12-3*x_5|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|^2*x_6*x_11^6*x_12^2+x_5^3*x_11^5*x_12^3,-x_6^3*x_7^3*x_9^5+3*x_4*x_6^2*x_7^2|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|*x_9^6-3*x_4^2*x_6*x_7*x_9^7+x_4^3*x_9^8,x_8^8*x_10^3-3*x_7*x_8^7*x_10^2*x_11|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|+3*x_7^2*x_8^6*x_10*x_11^2-x_7^3*x_8^5*x_11^3,x_2^5*x_3^3*x_11^3-3*x_2^6*x_3^|
|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
|2*x_11^2*x_12+3*x_2^7*x_3*x_11*x_12^2-x_2^8*x_12^3);                         |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i9 : time isCodimAtLeast(5, I, PairLimit => 5, Verbose=>true)                |
| -- used 0.00300116s (cpu); 0.00314031s (thread); 0s (gc)                    |
|isCodimAtLeast: Computing codim of monomials based on ideal generators.      |
|                                                                             |
|o9 = true                                                                    |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+
|i10 : time isCodimAtLeast(5, I, PairLimit => 200, Verbose=>false)            |
| -- used 0.00233385s (cpu); 0.00300832s (thread); 0s (gc)                    |
|                                                                             |
|o10 = true                                                                   |
+-----------------------------------------------------------------------------+

Notice in the first case the function returned null, because the depth of
search was not high enough.  It only computed codim 5 times.  The second
returned true, but it did so as soon as the answer was found (and before we hit
the PairLimit limit).

